---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { DownloadButton } from '@/components/features/DownloadButton';
import RelatedGuides from '@/components/ui/RelatedGuides.astro';
import ProductShot from '@/components/ui/ProductShot.astro';
import cuttingImg from '@/assets/screenshots/video-cutting-interface.png';
import { getCachedLatestRelease } from '@/utils/cache';
import { fallbackReleaseData } from '@/utils/fallback';

const release = (await getCachedLatestRelease()) ?? fallbackReleaseData;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Basketball Video Analyzer vs Kinovea"
  fullTitle="Basketball Video Analyzer vs Kinovea | Two Free Tools, Two Jobs"
  description="Kinovea and Basketball Video Analyzer are both free and open source. Kinovea measures movement on Windows; this one cuts and tags game film on Windows, Mac and Linux."
  type="article"
>
  <article class="py-20 sm:py-28">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="max-w-xl">
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold text-warm-900 leading-[1.1] mb-6">
        Basketball Video Analyzer vs Kinovea
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg sm:text-xl text-warm-700 mb-12">
        Both are free, both are open source, and coaches find both when they
        search for free video analysis. They answer different questions.
        Kinovea is for looking closely at one movement: a jump shot, a defensive
        slide, a landing. This app is for working through a whole game and
        coming out the other side with clips sorted by what happened.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-12 mb-4">
        What Kinovea is
      </h2>
      <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-5">
        Kinovea is a free, open-source program under the GPL v2 license. Its
        own description is a set of tools to capture, slow down, compare,
        annotate, track and measure motion in videos. You can play two videos
        side by side and sync them on a common moment, measure angles,
        distances and times by hand or with semi-automatic tracking, draw lines,
        arrows and shapes on the frame, and export the measurements as CSV. The
        current version is 2025.2. It runs on Windows 7 to 11, and its download
        page says it does not run on Apple Silicon Macs, even through Parallels.
      </p>
      <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-5">
        Kinovea's feature list does not mention tagging clips into categories
        or cutting a game into files. A shooting coach fixing a release point
        will not miss either: side-by-side comparison and an angle tool are
        what that job needs.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-12 mb-4">
        What this tool is
      </h2>
      <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-5">
        Basketball Video Analyzer is built around the game, not the single
        rep. Load the film, press Z and M to mark the start and end of each
        play, tag it with categories and players, and export the clips as video
        files in one folder per category. Each clip can carry a spot on the
        court, so the stats tab draws a shot chart, and you can draw on paused
        video and have the drawing burned into the exported clip. It runs on
        Windows, macOS and Linux and is open source under the AGPL.
      </p>

      <ProductShot
        src={cuttingImg}
        alt={"The app with the side panel and the bottom panel both hidden, leaving the video, the scrub bar, the transport row reading 36:20 of 1:25:39, and the Mark In (Z) and Mark Out (M) row."}
        caption={"Z marks the start of a play and M the end, so a game turns into a list of clips as you watch it."}
      />

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8 relative left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-[min(100vw-2rem,44rem)]">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-warm-300">
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-display font-semibold text-warm-900"></th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4 font-display font-semibold text-warm-900">Basketball Video Analyzer</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4 font-display font-semibold text-warm-900">Kinovea</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-warm-700">
            <tr class="border-b border-warm-200">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Price</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Free</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Free</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-warm-200">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">License</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">AGPL</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">GPL v2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-warm-200">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Platforms</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Windows, macOS, Linux</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Windows</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-warm-200">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Cut a game into tagged clips</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Yes</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Not in its feature list</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-warm-200">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Drawing on video</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Yes</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-warm-200">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Angle and distance measurement</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">No</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-warm-200">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Side-by-side comparison</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">No</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-warm-200">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium">Shot chart and clip stats</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">Yes</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4">No</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-warm-700 mb-8">
        Kinovea details from
        <a class="underline underline-offset-2" href="https://www.kinovea.org/" rel="noopener">kinovea.org</a>,
        its <a class="underline underline-offset-2" href="https://www.kinovea.org/features.html" rel="noopener">feature list</a>
        and its <a class="underline underline-offset-2" href="https://www.kinovea.org/download.html" rel="noopener">download page</a>,
        checked 16 November 2026.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-warm-900 mt-12 mb-4">
        Which one fits you
      </h2>
      <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-5">
        If you coach technique and want to slow a movement down, measure it
        and compare it with last month's, use Kinovea. If you break down games
        and scout opponents, and want a folder of pick-and-roll clips by Monday,
        use this one. They cost nothing, so plenty of coaches keep both.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-warm-300">
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-warm-900 mb-4">Try it free</h2>
        <p class="text-warm-700 mb-6 max-w-lg">
          Free and open source, on Windows, macOS and Linux.
        </p>
        <DownloadButton release={release} client:idle />
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <RelatedGuides containerClass="max-w-6xl" current="/vs-kinovea/" />

  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@graph": [
        {
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "url": "https://basketballvideoanalyzer.com/vs-kinovea/",
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Is Kinovea good for basketball film breakdown?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Kinovea is built for studying one movement: slow motion, side-by-side comparison, and angle and distance measurement. Its feature list does not mention tagging a game into clips. For breaking down whole games, Basketball Video Analyzer cuts and tags clips by category and player."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Does Kinovea run on a Mac?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Kinovea's download page lists Windows 7 to 11 and says it does not run on Apple Silicon Macs, even through Parallels (checked 16 November 2026). Basketball Video Analyzer runs on Windows, macOS and Linux."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Are both free?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Yes. Kinovea is free and open source under the GPL v2. Basketball Video Analyzer is free and open source under the AGPL."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
          "itemListElement": [
            { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://basketballvideoanalyzer.com/" },
            { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "vs Kinovea", "item": "https://basketballvideoanalyzer.com/vs-kinovea/" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
